item.description:
arXiv:2604.01686v3 Announce Type: replace Abstract: We study kinematical properties of the process $l^-l^+ 	o 
u ar{
u}Zh$ at future TeV-scale lepton colliders. We classify the Feynman amplitudes into three main topological categories: vector-boson scattering, $l^-W^+$ scattering, and $W^-l^+$ scattering contributions, and analyze the interference patterns among these amplitudes in both the recently proposed Feynman-diagram (FD) gauge and the conventional unitary gauge. We demonstrate that the rapidity distributions of the $Z$ boson closely resemble those in $l^-l^+ 	o 
u ar{
u}Z$, while the Higgs-boson rapidity distribution is significantly different: Higgs bosons are produced nearly isotropically, whereas longitudinally polarized $Z$ bosons are enhanced in the forward and backward regions. We find that this difference is traced to the topology-dependent structure of the FD-gauge amplitudes. We also demonstrate that suitable kinematical cuts can isolate specific subamplitudes, enabling partial FD-gauge amplitudes to effectively describe physical distributions.
